      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Isoflurane has shown better control of intensive care sedation than propofol or midazolam and seems to be a useful alternative. However, its effect on survival remains unclear."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The objective of this study is to compare mortality after sedation with either isoflurane or propofol/midazolam."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "A retrospective analysis of data in a hospital database for a cohort of consecutive patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Sixteen-bed interdisciplinary surgical ICU of a German university hospital."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "Consecutive cohort of 369 critically ill surgical patients defined within the database of the hospital information system. All patients were continuously ventilated and sedated for more than 96\u200ah between 1 January 2005 and 31 December 2010. After excluding 169 patients (93 >79 years old, 10 <40 years old, 46 mixed sedation, 20 lost to follow-up), 200 patients were studied, 72 after isoflurane and 128 after propofol/midazolam."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "Sedation with isoflurane using the AnaConDa system compared with intravenous sedation with propofol or midazolam."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Hospital mortality (primary) and 365-day mortality (secondary) were compared with the Kaplan-Meier analysis and a log-rank test. Adjusted odds ratios (ORs) [with 95% confidence interval (95% CI)] were calculated by logistic regression analyses to determine the risk of death after isoflurane sedation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "After sedation with isoflurane, the in-hospital mortality and 365-day mortality were significantly lower than after propofol/midazolam sedation: 40 versus 63% (P\u200a=\u200a0.005) and 50 versus 70% (P\u200a=\u200a0.013), respectively. After adjustment for potential confounders (coronary heart disease, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, acute renal failure, creatinine, age and Simplified Acute Physiology Score II), patients after isoflurane were at a lower risk of death during their hospital stay (OR 0.35; 95% CI 0.18 to 0.68, P\u200a=\u200a0.002) and within the first 365 days (OR 0.41; 95% CI 0.21 to 0.81, P\u200a=\u200a0.010)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Compared with propofol/midazolam sedation, long-term sedation with isoflurane seems to be well tolerated in this group of critically ill patients after surgery."
        }
      ]},
